# Link-check exclusions for marlowfernandez.com
#
# One regex per line. Every entry needs a reason: an unexplained exclusion is
# indistinguishable from a silently broken link, and `team.md` names "links
# resolve" as a required CI gate.

# LinkedIn returns HTTP 999 to any client that is not a real browser. It is a
# bot-detection response, not a broken link — the URL resolves fine for a human.
# Left in, it would fail every run and make a required gate permanently red.
#
# Residual risk, stated rather than hidden: a typo in this URL would NOT be
# caught by CI. It is one hand-verified link, and it is the only external
# destination on the site.
^https?://(www\.)?linkedin\.com/

# The site's own canonical URL, emitted into every page's `<link rel=canonical>`
# and Open Graph tags by `metadataBase`.
#
# Excluding this breaks a genuine deadlock rather than dodging a failure: the
# link check runs in `verify`, `deploy` runs only after `verify` passes, and the
# domain does not resolve until a deploy has succeeded. Checked, the first run
# would fail on a URL that cannot exist yet — and would keep failing, because
# the deploy that would make it resolve is gated behind the check itself.
#
# `deployment-execution` (4.3) verifies both live domains directly with curl,
# which is the correct place for a liveness check on a deployed URL.
^https?://(www\.)?marlowfernandez\.com

# Same reasoning for the redirect domain, which `deployment-pipeline` (4.1)
# configures and `deployment-execution` (4.3) verifies returns 301.
^https?://(www\.)?marlow\.software
